      "hasMediaInOriginal": true,
      "fallbackReason": null,
      "text": "Beirut Cease-Fire™: Loaded, Aimed, Still “Paused”\n\nIsrael hits the southern outskirts of Beirut for the first time since Washington sold its latest Lebanon truce, and everybody pretends they’re still “close to a deal.” \n\nThe strike targeted Dahiyeh — Hezbollah turf — officially as retaliation for rockets fired into Israel, hours before Iran answered with missiles of its own at Israeli targets. \n\nThe “cease-fire” survives on paper; on the ground it’s just slower murder with better press releases.\n\nTehran keeps saying any peace with the U.S. is impossible while Israel is bombing Lebanon and refusing to withdraw. \n\nSo it fires from long range, Hezbollah fires from next door, and Iranian officials talk on X about “decisive and painful” responses and the “language of power.” \n\nIsrael responds through an anonymous official saying an Iranian attack would be an “opportunity to renew the campaign.” Translation: both sides claim they want a deal and openly salivate over an excuse to hit harder.\n\nTrump, watching the war between holes of golf in New Jersey, boils the whole thing down to a reality‑show threat: “We’re very close to a deal, or I’m going to blow the hell out of them.” \n\nAt the same time he leans on Netanyahu, curses him on the phone, demands Israel scale back in Lebanon — and Israel keeps striking anyway. \n\nWashington wants a clean, photo‑friendly agreement; Jerusalem wants strategic depth and Hezbollah broken; \n\nTehran wants sanctions relief, control over Hormuz and recognition of its regional muscle. None of that fits into one cease‑fire PDF.\n\nMeanwhile Hormuz stays half‑shut, ships move under escort, U.S. jets hit Iranian radar sites, Iranian missiles fly at U.S. bases in Kuwait and Bahrain, and Gulf civilians listen to sirens and “no casualties” statements. \n\nThe war is officially “paused,” but the region is living in a permanent rehearsal for the next round: limited strikes, blocked ports, frozen assets and negotiators arguing over who gets to say “we won” on TV.\n\nCall it managed escalation, call it deterrence — the mechanics are the same. Israel tests how far it can push in Lebanon under an American umbrella. Iran tests how much it can hit U.S. assets and shipping without triggering a full restart.\n\n Trump tests how long he can threaten to “blow the hell out of them” before markets, allies and voters panic. The only people not testing anything are the ones already under rubble or under curfew, watching great powers turn their cities into bargaining chips.\n\n#Israel #Lebanon #Iran #Trump #Netanyahu #Hezbollah #Hormuz #war #oligarchy #fakeDemocracy\n\n📱 American Оbserver - Stay up to date on all important events 🇺🇸",
